   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to clinical laboratory\nsupervision",
    "basePrintNo": "S7521",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7521-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 58,
       "fullName": "Thomas F. O'Mara",
       "imgName": "415_thomas_f._o'mara.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 415,
       "sessionMemberId": 941,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "O'MARA"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §576-d, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7521\n\nSPONSOR: HANNON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nclinical laboratory supervision\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to statutorily establish the requirements\nfor clinical laboratory supervision as well as the required qualifica-\ntions for clinical laboratory supervisors.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the public health by adding a new section\n576-d that would establish supervision requirements for a clinical lab,\nand would establish the required qualifications of a clinical lab super-\nvisor, to be consistent with the required qualifications of a clinical\nlab supervisor under federal Clinical Lab Improvement Act (CLIA)\nrequirements.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nNone. The current requirements for clinical lab supervision and clinical\nlab supervisors are contained in New York's Codes, Rules and Regu-\nlations.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, the clinical lab supervision requirements and the required\nqualifications of a clinical lab supervisor laid out in New York's\nCodes, Rules, and Regulations greatly exceed the requirements contained\nin the federal Clinical Improvement Act (CLIA), which is one year of\nexperience. These requirements, requiring a qualified supervisor to be\non site at all times and that clinical lab supervisors have at 2-6 years\nof experience, depending on degree/training, poses a severe burden on\nclinical labs operated by smaller upstate community hospitals, especial-\nly those located in geographically isolated rural areas with smaller\npopulations. These clinical labs have severe difficulty in recruiting\nand hiring qualified employees to staff their labs, which must operate\n24 hours a day to perform stat and critical value, and routine lab\ntests.\n\nFaced with this problem which has become of critical importance, clin-\nical labs unable to recruit qualified staff are either forced to operate\nout of compliance, facing potential DOH sanctions or to hire contract\nstaff at a cost of 2-3 times that of an employee which can create a\nfinancial hardship. Unless New York State changes its requirements to be\nmore consistent with national and federal standards, the only labs able\nto meet the NYS staffing standard will be large commercial labs (e.g.\nLabcorp, Metpath Laboratories, and Quest Diagnostics etc.). The hospi-\ntals in these areas would be force to outsource their lab activity to\nthese commercial labs which could have a deleterious effect on the qual-\nity. Furthermore, the closure of the hospital based labs could also\nhinder access to care if the hospital bases lab is performing testing\nfor community based providers. Finally, the closure of these hospital\n\nlabs would have a negative economic impact as jobs and revenue are driv-\nen out of the hospital and the community.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill is effective immediately.\n",
