    "lawCode": "Add §576-d, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7521A\n\nSPONSOR: HANNON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to clinical laborato-\nry supervision\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend the Public Health Law, in relation to statutorily establishing\nthe requirements for clinical laboratory supervision.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends the Public Health Law by adding a new\nsection 576-d that would establish supervision requirements for a clin-\nical laboratory.\n\nSection two of the bill establishes the effective date. JUSTIFICATION:\nCurrently, the clinical lab supervision requirements laid out in New\nYork's Codes, Rules, and Regulations greatly exceed the requirements\ncontained in the federal Clinical Improvement Act (CLIA). The require-\nment that a supervisor be physically present at all times poses a severe\nburden on clinical labs operated both by smaller upstate community\nhospitals- especially in rural locations- and urban hospital systems\nwith labs at multiple sites. These clinical labs find severe difficulty\nin maintaining the adequate staff to operate labs 24 hours a day, as is\nnecessary to perform critical tests.\n\nFaced with this problem, clinical labs are forced to either operate out\nof compliance or hire contract staff at a cost of 2-3 times that of an\nemployee. This bill would authorize supervision via phone or synchronous\n2-way AV communication, pursuant to regulations by the Department, and\ncap the number of labs a supervisor can oversee at five.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
